"The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.23291)\nciteturn5academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Title: The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False ... Noel\nTitle: The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False\nAuthors: Jonathan A. Noel\nDate: Sun Sep 20 01:58:08 2026\n\nWe exhibit a graph $G$ with $14$ vertices and list chromatic number equal to $3$ such that there is a $2$-list assignment $L$ of $G$ such that at most $9$ vertices of $G$ can be properly coloured from $L$. This disproves the Partial List Colouring Conjecture of Albertson, Grossman and Haas. This counterexample was discovered and fully verified by ChatGPT 6 Astra Ultra after some persistent prompting, but almost no mathematical input, from the author.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False | ArxivLens (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/the-partial-list-colouring-conjecture-is-false-9696-5955cc96)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Back ArXiv math.CO The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False ... # The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False\n\nBack ArXiv math.CO The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False\n\nArXiv math.CO\n\n# The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False\n\nJonathan A. Noel\n\n2609.23291 Published Sep 20, 2026\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: Sep 20, 2026\n\nCategories: math.CO\n\n### Metrics\n\nSource: ArXiv\n\n### Paper Preview\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe exhibit a graph $G$ with $14$ vertices and list chromatic number equal to $3$ such that there is a $2$-list assignment $L$ of $G$ such that at most $9$ vertices of $G$ can be properly coloured from $L$. This disproves the Partial List Colouring Conjecture of Albertson, Grossman and Haas. This counterexample was discovered and fully verified by ChatGPT 6 Astra Ultra after some persistent prompting, but almost no mathematical input, from the author.\n\n### Paper Details\n\nPaper ID: 2609.23291\n\nComments: 4 pages, 1 figure\n\nCategories:\n\nmath.CO\n\nView on arXiv Download PDF\n\n### Authors\n\nJA Jonathan A. Noel 39 papers--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJonathan Noel (https://jonathannoel.ca/)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The List Total Colouring Conjecture is False AI ... The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False AI ... Turán Colourings in Off-Diagonal Ramsey Multiplicity ... Mixing Homomorphisms, Recolorings, and Extending Circular Precolorings\n## Full Publication List\n\nI disclose any use of artificial intelligence (AI) in my papers through an AI Declaration and/or other acknowledgement. The AI badge marks papers involving substantial intellectual contributions from AI, beyond assistance such as proofreading or computational support.\n\nShowing 43 publications.\n\n### Submitted\n\n43. The List Total Colouring Conjecture is False AI\n\nSolo paper. Submitted\n\nGraph colouring\n\n42. The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False AI\n\nSolo paper. Submitted\n\nGraph colouring\n\n41. Orthogonal Pairs in Maps from the Sphere to the Circle AI\n\nSolo paper. Submitted\n\nGraph colouring\n\n40. Sharp bounds for the fractional chromatic number of high-girth d-degenerate graphs\n\nwith Peter Allen and Abhishek Dhawan. Submitted\n\nGraph colouring Probability theory Algorithms and complexity\n\n39. On Tournament Anti-Sidorenko Orientations of Trees\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Sperner-Type Theorem for Set-Partition Systems (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=5ce83117fc60510bfb336d4c369bea072fe390a3&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; A Sperner-Type Theorem for Set-Partition Systems\nKaren Meagher ... Lucia Moura ... Brett Stevens ... A Sperner partition system is a system of set partitions such that any two set\nA Sperner-Type Theorem for Set-Partition Systems\nKaren Meagher\nDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics\nUniversity of Ottawa, Ottawa, Ontario, Canada\nkmeagher@site.uottawa.ca\nLucia Moura\nSchool of Information Technology and Engineering\nUniversity of Ottawa, Ottawa, Ontario, Canada\nlucia@site.uottawa.ca\nBrett Stevens\nSchool of Mathematics and Statistics\nCarleton University, Ottawa, Ontario, Canada\nbrett@math.carleton.ca\nSubmitted: Aug 15, 2005; Accepted: Oct 26, 2005; Published: Oct 31, 2005\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcations: 05D05\nAbstract\nA Sperner partition system is a system of set partitions such that any two set\npartitions P and Q in the system have the property that for all classes A of P and\nall classes B of Q, A ̸⊆B and B ̸⊆A. A k-partition is a set partition with k\nclasses and a k-partition is said to be uniform if every class has the same cardinality\nc = n/k. In this paper, we prove a higher order generalization of Sperner’s Theorem.\nIn particular, we show that if k divides n the largest Sperner k-partition system on\nan n-set has cardinality\n\u0000 n−1\nn/k−1\n\u0001\nand is a uniform partition system. We give a bound\non the cardinality of a Sperner k-partition system of an n-set for any k and n.\n1\nIntroduction\nIn this paper, we prove a Sperner-type theorem for systems of set partitions and re-\nlated results. These theorems are stated after some notation and background results are\nintroduced.\nFor i, j positive integers with i ≤j, let [i, j] denote the set {i, i + 1, . . . , j}. For k, n\npositive integers, set\n\u0000[n]\nk\n\u0001\n= {A ⊆[1, n] : |A| = k}. This set is also known as a complete\nk-uniform hypergraph. A system A of subsets of [1, n] is said to be k-uniform if A ⊆\n\u0000[n]\nk\n\u0001\n.\nthe electronic journal of combinatorics 12 (2005), #N20\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSperner partition systems (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=99ac0e4d7f747475693e145981fd333abaead18a&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn5search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; A Sperner k-partition system on a set X is a set of partitions of X into k classes ... Meagher, Moura and Stevens in [2] who showed that if |X| = kℓ, then the largest Sperner ... In 1928 Sperner gave the exact size of the largest Sperner set system.\nSperner partition systems\nKaren Meagher†\nP. C. Li∗\nDept. of Mathematics and Statistics\nUniversity of Regina\nRegina, Saskatchewan\nCanada S4S 0A2\nDept. of Computer Science\nUniversity of Manitoba\nWinnipeg, Manitoba\nCanada R3T 2N2\nDecember 21, 2013\nAbstract\nA Sperner k-partition system on a set X is a set of partitions of X into k classes\nsuch that the classes of the partitions form a Sperner set system (so no class from a\npartition is a subset of a class from another partition). These systems were deﬁned by\nMeagher, Moura and Stevens in [2] who showed that if |X| = kℓ, then the largest Sperner\nk-partition system has size 1\nk\n\u0000|X|\nℓ\n\u0001\n. In this paper we ﬁnd bounds on the size of the largest\nSperner k-partition system where k does not divide the size of X, speciﬁcally, we give\nan exact bound when k = 2 and upper and lower bounds when |X| = 2k+1, |X| = 2k+2\nand |X| = 3k −1.\n1\nIntroduction\narXiv:1201.4375v1  [math.CO]  20 Jan 2012\nThis paper continues the work from [2] that established Sperner type theorems for partitions.\nTwo subsets from a set X are call incomparable if neither set is contained in the other. A\nSperner set system on a set X is a collection of subsets of X such that any two are incompa-\nrable. In 1928 Sperner gave the exact size of the largest Sperner set system.\n1.1 Theorem. (Sperner’s Theorem [3]) A Sperner set system on a set X with cardinality n\nhas at most\n\u0000\nn\n⌊n/2⌋\n\u0001\nsets. The only Sperner set systems that meet this bound are the collection\nof all sets of size ⌊n/2⌋or the collection of all sets of size ⌈n/2⌉.\n∗Research supported by NSERC Discovery Grant 250389-06\n†Research supported by NSERC Discovery Grant 341214-08\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKAREN MEAGHER, University of Regina (https://www2.cms.math.ca/Events/summer12/abs/pdf/com-km.pdf)\nciteturn5search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.6 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; KAREN MEAGHER, University of Regina ... In 2005 Lucia Moura, Brett Stevens and I proved a bound on the size of a Sperner partition system and showed that this bound can be met if the size of the classes in the partitions are all equal.\nKAREN MEAGHER, University of Regina  \n*Sperner partition systems*\n\nA Sperner set system is a set system in which no set is a subset of another. Sperner proved that the maximum size of a Sperner set system on {1, 2, . . . , n} is \\(\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor}\\) and the only systems meeting this bound are the middle levels of the poset of sets ordered by inclusion. A *Sperner partition system* is a set of partitions with the property that no class from one partition is the subset of a class from another partition. A Sperner partition system can be considered to be a resolvable Sperner set system. In 2005 Lucia Moura, Brett Stevens and I proved a bound on the size of a Sperner partition system and showed that this bound can be met if the size of the classes in the partitions are all equal. Recently, P. C. Li and I determined bounds on the sizes of some Sperner partition systems when the sizes of the classes cannot be all the same.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSperner Partition Systems - Li - 2013 - Journal of Combinatorial Designs - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcd.21330)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; These systems were defined by Meagher, Moura, and Stevens in [6] who showed that if Image: urn:x-wiley:10638539:media:jcd21330:jcd21330-math-0001, then the largest Sperner k-partition system has size Image: urn:x-wiley:10638539:media:jcd21330:jcd21330-math-0002. ... Stevens, A Sperner-type theorem for set-partition systems, Electron J Combin 12(20) (2005), 6 pp.\n\nVolume 21, Issue 7 pp. 267-279\n\nResearch Article\n\n# Sperner Partition Systems\n\nP. C. Li, \n\nP. C. Li\n\nDepartment of Computer Science, University of Manitoba, Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada, R3T 2N2\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nKaren Meagher, \n\nKaren Meagher\n\n  * kmeagher@math.uregina.ca\n\nDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics, University of Regina, Regina, Saskatchewan, Canada, S4S 0A2\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nP. C. Li, \n\nP. C. Li\n\nDepartment of Computer Science, University of Manitoba, Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada, R3T 2N2\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nKaren Meagher, \n\nKaren Meagher\n\n  * kmeagher@math.uregina.ca\n\nDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics, University of Regina, Regina, Saskatchewan, Canada, S4S 0A2\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nFirst published: 16 August 2012\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jcd.21330Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\nContract grant sponsor: NSERC Discovery; contract grant number: 250389-06 (P. C. L); Contract grant sponsor: NSERC Discovery; contract grant number: 341214-08 (K. M.).\n\nRead the full text\n\n### Abstract\n\nA Spernerk-partition system on a set X is a set of partitions of X into k classes such that the classes of the partitions form a Sperner set system (so no class from a partition is a subset of a class from another partition). These systems were defined by Meagher, Moura, and Stevens in [6] who showed that if Image: urn:x-wiley:10638539:media:jcd21330:jcd21330-math-0001, then the largest Sperner k-partition system has size Image: urn:x-wiley:10638539:media:jcd21330:jcd21330-math-0002. In this paper, we find bounds on the size of the largest Sperner k-partition system where k does not divide the size of X, specifically, we give upper and lower bounds when Image: urn:x-wiley:10638539:media:jcd21330:jcd21330-math-0003, Image: urn:x-wiley:10638539:media:jcd21330:jcd21330-math-0004 and Image: urn:x-wiley:10638539:media:jcd21330:jcd21330-math-0005.\n\n## \n\nReferences\n\n  * 1 C. Colbourn and J. Dinitz (Editors). Handbook of Combinatorial Designs, Discrete Mathematics and its Applications, 2nd ed. Chapman & Hall/CRC, Boca Raton, FL, 2007.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  * 2 P. Erdős, C. Ko, and R. Rado, Intersection theorems for systems of finite sets, Quart J Math Oxford Ser 12(2), (1961), 313–320.\n\nWeb of Science® Google Scholar\n\n  * 3 P. Erdős and L. Székely, “Erdős-Ko-Rado theorems of higher order,” in Numbers, Information and Complexity, I. Althöfer, Cai Ning, G. Dueck, L. H. Khachatrian, M. Pinsker, G. Sarkozy, I. Wegener, and Z. Zhang, (Editors), Kluwer Academic Publishers, Boston, MA, 2000, pp. 117–124.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  * 4 L. Lovász, Combinatorial Problems and Exercises, 2nd ed., North-Holland Publishing Co., Amsterdam, 1993.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  * 5 K. Meagher and L. Moura, Erdős-Ko-Rado theorems for uniform set-partition systems, Electron J Combin 12(1) (2005), 12 pp. (Research Paper 40).\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  * 6 K. Meagher, L. Moura, B. Stevens, A Sperner-type theorem for set-partition systems, Electron J Combin 12(20) (2005), 6 pp. (Research Note).\n\nVolume21, Issue7\n\nJuly 2013\n\nPages 267-279\n\n### How to cite\n\nLi, P.C. and Meagher, K. (2013), Sperner Partition Systems. J. Combin. Designs, 21: 267-279. https://doi.org/10.1002/jcd.21330--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Papierüberprüfung] The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False (https://www.themoonlight.io/de/review/the-partial-list-colouring-conjecture-is-false)\nciteturn5search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ## [Papierüberprüfung] The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False ... The paper provides a disproof of the Partial List Colouring Conjecture, which posited that for a graph $G$G with $n$n vertices and list chromatic number $\\chi_l(G) = k$χ l​(G)=k, one could always properly $L$L-colour at least $n(\\ell/k)$n(ℓ/k) vertices for any $\\ell$ℓ-list assignment $L$L, where $1 \\le \\ell < k$1≤ℓ<k.\n\n## [Papierüberprüfung] The Partial List Colouring Conjecture is False\n\nOriginalpapier Review\n\nImage: review-image\n\nThe paper provides a disproof of the Partial List Colouring Conjecture, which posited that for a graph $G$G with $n$n vertices and list chromatic number $\\chi_l(G) = k$χ l​(G)=k, one could always properly $L$L-colour at least $n(\\ell/k)$n(ℓ/k) vertices for any $\\ell$ℓ-list assignment $L$L, where $1 \\le \\ell < k$1≤ℓ<k.\n\n### Core Construction\n\nThe author constructs a counterexample graph $G$G with $|V(G)| = 14$∣V(G)∣=14 and $\\chi_l(G) = 3$χ l​(G)=3. The graph consists of four disjoint triangles $T_1, T_2, T_3, T_4$T 1​,T 2​,T 3​,T 4​, each containing a \"private vertex\" $p_i$p i​, and two additional non-adjacent terminal vertices $s_1$s 1​ and $s_2$s 2​. All non-private vertices in each triangle $T_i$T i​ are connected to both $s_1$s 1​ and $s_2$s 2​.\n\nThe 2-list assignment $L$L is defined as follows:\n\n  * $L(s_1) = \\{1, 2\\}$L(s 1​)={1,2} and $L(s_2) = \\{3, 4\\}$L(s 2​)={3,4}.\n  * Triangles are assigned lists $L(T_1) = \\{1, 3\\}$L(T 1​)={1,3}, $L(T_2) = \\{1, 4\\}$L(T 2​)={1,4}, $L(T_3) = \\{2, 3\\}$L(T 3​)={2,3}, and $L(T_4) = \\{2, 4\\}$L(T 4​)={2,4}.\n\n### Methodology and Proof\n\nTo demonstrate that the conjecture fails, the author establishes two primary claims:\n\n  1. Maximum Colourable Vertices: With the specified list assignment $L$L, the maximum number of vertices that can be properly coloured is 9. Since the conjecture predicts a lower bound of $n(\\ell/k) = 14(2/3) \\approx 9.33$n(ℓ/k)=14(2/3)≈9.33, the result $9 < 9.33$9<9.33 invalidates the hypothesis. The proof relies on the observation that each triangle $T_i$T i​ can have at most two vertices coloured. Because the terminals $s_1$s 1​ and $s_2$s 2​ are connected to all non-private vertices of each triangle, if both terminals are coloured, at least one triangle $T_i$T i​ must have one of its colours blocked by the choice at $s_1$s 1​ or $s_2$s 2​, restricting the total number of colourable vertices in that triangle to at most one. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPROGRAMA DE APOYO A PROYECTOS DE INVESTIGACIÓN E INNOVACIÓN TECNOLÓGICA (https://dgapa.unam.mx/images/papiit/transparencia/proyxanio/2025/segundo/IG100822_Informe_Final_2022.pdf)\nciteturn5search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Título de la ponencia On the detection of local and global amoebas: theoretical insights and practical\nParticipantes\nADRIANA HANSBERG PASTOR Organizador\nAMANDA MONTEJANO CANTORAL Organizador\nDENAE VENTURA ARREDONDO Ponente\nTítulo de la ponencia On the detection of local and global amoebas: theoretical insights and practical\nalgorithms\nNombre del evento XII Latin-American Algorithms, Graphs and Optimization Symposium\nLugar Huatulco País México Difusión Internacional\nParticipantes\nADRIANA HANSBERG PASTOR Organizador\nAMANDA MONTEJANO CANTORAL Organizador\nJORGE RENÉ GONZÁLEZ MARTÍNEZ Ponente\nTítulo de la ponencia Patrones inevitables y amibas\nNombre del evento XIII Encuentro Andaluz de Matemática Discreta\nLugar Cádiz País España Difusión Internacional\nParticipantes\nADRIANA HANSBERG PASTOR Ponente\nTítulo de la ponencia Una caja, una casa, un palacio y un castillo: cuatro teoremas extremales\nNombre del evento Coloquio Queretano de Matemáticas\nLugar Instituto de Matemáticas Unidad Juriquilla, Querétaro País México Difusión Nacional\nParticipantes\nAMANDA MONTEJANO CANTORAL Ponente\nTítulo de la ponencia Gráficas de link constante y números de Turán inducido\nNombre del evento VI Encuentro conjunto de la Real Sociedad Matemática Española y la Sociedad\nMatemática Mexicana\nLugar Valencia País E